    },
    {
      "id": "def-omt-semantics-m4-p087-t02-r3",
      "term": "Note 3",
      "text": "Merit raises are not provided according to any regular time interval; they are provided on a supervi- sor’s recommendation based on evidence of exceptional effort and performance.",
      "layout_body": null,
      "leading_gap": null,
      "source": {
        "path": "sections/semantic/clause-4c/page-087.tex",
        "line": 123,
        "end_line": null
      },
      "clause_id": "clause-4"
    },
    {
      "id": "def-omt-semantics-m4-p087-t02-r4",
      "term": "Note 4",
      "text": "Years of service are a factor in any merit raise. This value is only changed on the anniversary of the employee’s hire.",
      "layout_body": null,
      "leading_gap": null,
      "source": {
        "path": "sections/semantic/clause-4c/page-087.tex",
        "line": 131,
        "end_line": null
      },
      "clause_id": "clause-4"
    },
    {
      "id": "def-omt-semantics-m4-p087-t02-r5",
      "term": "Note 5",
      "text": "Sometimes a dish can be modified to be gluten- or wheat-free, e.g. gluten-free pasta can be used for some pasta dishes, but dishes like ravioli and lasagna are usually pre-made with wheat pasta and can’t be modified.",
      "layout_body": null,
      "leading_gap": null,
      "source": {
        "path": "sections/semantic/clause-4c/page-087.tex",
        "line": 139,
        "end_line": null
      },
      "clause_id": "clause-4"
    },
    {
      "id": "def-omt-semantics-m4-p087-t02-r6",
      "term": "Note 6",
      "text": "Gluten Normalization Function: 1) No—Ingredients contain wheat and those ingredients can’t be modified 2) Intolerant—Ingredients can be modified to remove wheat, rye, or barley (cross-contamination free environment is N/A) 3) Celiac —Ingredients can be modified to remove wheat, rye, or barley; prepared in cross-contami- nation free environment 4) Allergy—Ingredients can be modified to remove wheat; prepared in cross-contamination free en- vironment 5) All—No ingredients contain wheat, rye, or barley; prepared in cross-contamination free environ- ment",
      "layout_body": null,
      "leading_gap": null,
      "source": {
        "path": "sections/semantic/clause-4c/page-087.tex",
        "line": 147,
        "end_line": null
      },
      "clause_id": "clause-4"
    },
    {
      "id": "def-omt-semantics-m4-p087-t02-r7",
      "term": "Note 7",
      "text": "Gluten is found in three grains: wheat, barley, and rye. Gluten intolerant individuals will have varying degrees of negative, but not necessarily debilitating, reactions to these foods. Individuals with Celiac disease will have debilitating reactions to these foods and suffer long-term, potentially life-threatening effects. Those with wheat allergies may suffer immediate, life-threatening reactions, but may be able to eat barley and rye. Depending on levels of sensitivity, cross-contamination in the kitchen can be dangerous to the extent that separate food preparation areas and tools are required.",
      "layout_body": null,
      "leading_gap": null,
      "source": {
        "path": "sections/semantic/clause-4c/page-087.tex",
        "line": 155,
        "end_line": null
      },
      "clause_id": "clause-4"
    },
